      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "DiGeorge syndrome is characterized by conotruncal cardiac defects, hypocalcemia, and a hypoplastic thymus. Many, but not all, patients have a heterozygous deletion of chromosome 22q11.2. In its most severe form, it represents a devastating syndrome with high mortality. Patients with severe immunodeficiency are candidates for a thymic transplant or a fully matched bone marrow transplant. Fortunately, the majority of patients with either DiGeorge syndrome or chromosome 22q11.2 deletion syndrome have a mild to moderate immunodeficiency. These patients may develop recurrent infections or autoimmune disease."},
